## Intuition

The key idea behind solving this problem is Dynamic Programming (DP). At each step, we have three possible choices: climb 1, 2, or 3 stairs. These choices form a decision tree, where each node represents a possible position and each branch represents a choice we can make. Since this tree can contain overlapping subproblems, we can use DP to avoid recalculating the same results multiple times.

## Approach

1. Initialize a DP vector of size n + 1 and set the first element dp[0] to 0.
2. Iterate from 1 to n, calculating the number of ways to reach each stair using the three possible previous positions.
3. Take the minimum cost among the three possible previous positions to determine the minimum cost of reaching the current stair.
4. return dp[-1]

## Complexity

Time Complexity: O(n) — We iterate through the DP vector once, performing a constant number of operations at each iteration.
Space Complexity: O(n) — We use a DP vector of size n + 1 to store the minimum costs.
